     "<br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Em dom, 23 de set de 2018 às 13:59, Phil Chalmers &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">rphilip....@gmail.com</a>&gt; escreveu:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">At this stage of your theory, I would probably recommend not using IRT due to its computational complexity. You might want to try a SEM (e..g, via lavaan) approach first to determine what approximately works well, and make decisions from there. It&#39;s possible to go about these analyses using IRT, but the estimation times will be noticeably large and likely less flexible than more developed alternatives. HTH. <div><br clear=\"all\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\" data-smartmail=\"gmail_signature\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Phil</div></div></div></div><br></div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\">On Tue, Sep 18, 2018 at 10:03 PM Hugo Harada &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">hugo....@adaptativa.com.br</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\"><div>Hello all,</div><div><br></div><div>I need some help with longitudinal measurements. I am trying to model latent trait of individuals using items, designed to measure the same trait but completely different items, at different moments. I am considering three different types of models as you can see in the attached figure. I would like to simultaneously estimate item and individual parameters and make sure that all are in the same scale. Measurements will be done months apart. Hopefully, I would like NOT to use common itens in the measurements.Is this possible?</div><div><br></div><div>My understanding is that</div><div><br></div><div>With Model 1, the scale of the items in moments 1 and 2 will <b>be different</b>. Is this correct?</div><div><br></div><div>With Model 2, the scale of the items in moments 1 and 2 will <b>be the same</b>. Is this correct?</div><div><br></div><div>With Model 3, the scale of the items in moments 1 and 2 will <b>be the same</b>. The assumption here is that the items will be equated with the linking test. Is this correct? If yes, how can  I pass these info to MIRT?</div><div><br></div>Any insight or references will help me greatly and will be highly welcome!<div><br></div><div>Looking forward to hearing from you,</div><div><br></div><div>Hugo <br><div> </div></div></div><div><br></div>-- <br><div dir=\"ltr\" data-smartmail=\"gmail_signature\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div><div><br><table width=\"100%\" border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&#39;Times New Roman&#39;;font-size:medium\"><tbody><tr><td align=\"left\" valign=\"middle\" width=\"10\"><p style=\"margin-right:10px;font-family:Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:12px;line-height:14px\"><a href=\"http://www.adaptativa.com.br/\" style=\"text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.adaptativa.com.br/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792473337315000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3_4gdH5BFZLGQRinmKJ_4v\"><img src=\"https://ci4.googleusercontent.com/proxy/FE2I4wjFnN2cAFKRmI3TKMszOlSp21wnNu4SqBeCJZBTRvT_FvrlgmwCaxvFTesKdXjysxxb7nvH4if6T_hi884vh3N59BHtgm3-wqx9bgkcnTo-Jw1Bg01c1Tk3qGCKYDUC3lEyIIXfgYCyCshNyEk3Ee8Wg4ibOcS9FfxhtyTI=s0-d-e1-ft#https://htmlsigs.s3.amazonaws.com/logos/files/000/177/391/landscape/adaptativa_256px80pxL_72dpi_SEM_SLOGAN.jpg\" alt=\"Adaptativa\" border=\"0\" height=\"62\" width=\"200\"></a></p></td><td align=\"left\"><p style=\"font-family:Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;line-height:14px;color:rgb(33,33,33)\"><font size=\"2\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold;display:inline\">Hugo Harada</span><spa